    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000049",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Explaining the Trend in Reducing Ability of Halide Ions",
    "reading_order" : 49,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "heading",
    "heading_level" : 4,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000050",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "As you go down Group 7, the halide ions get bigger (Cl⁻ → Br⁻ → I⁻)",
    "reading_order" : 50,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000051",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "So, the attraction between the outer electrons and the nucleus gets weaker",
    "reading_order" : 51,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000052",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "This makes it easier for the larger halide ions to lose electrons",
    "reading_order" : 52,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000053",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "When they lose electrons, they reduce other substances,",
    "reading_order" : 53,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000054",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "So the reducing power increases down the group:",
    "reading_order" : 54,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000055",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Cl⁻ < Br⁻ < I⁻",
    "reading_order" : 55,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000056",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Silver ions & ammonia",
    "reading_order" : 56,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "heading",
    "heading_level" : 4,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000057",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Halide ions can be identified in an **unknown** **solution** by dissolving the solution in **nitric acid **and then adding a **silver nitrate solution **followed by **ammonia **solution",
    "reading_order" : 57,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000058",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The halide ions will react with the silver nitrate solution as follows:",
    "reading_order" : 58,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000059",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**AgNO**<sub>**3 **</sub>**(aq) + X**<sup>**- **</sup>**(aq) → AgX (s) + NO**<sub>**3**</sub><sup>**- **</sup>**(aq)**",
    "reading_order" : 59,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000060",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**Ag**<sup>**+ **</sup>**(aq) + X**<sup>**- **</sup>**(aq) → AgX (s)**",
    "reading_order" : 60,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000061",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**X**<sup>**-**</sup> is the halide ion in both equations",
    "reading_order" : 61,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000062",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "If the unknown solution contains halide ions, then a **precipitate** of the **silver halide** will be formed (AgX)",
    "reading_order" : 62,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000064",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "***A silver halide precipitate is formed upon addition of silver nitrate solution to halide ion solution***",
    "reading_order" : 64,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000065",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**Dilute **followed by **concentrated ammonia** is added to the silver halide solution to identify the halide ion",
    "reading_order" : 65,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000066",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "If the precipitate dissolves in **dilute **ammonia the unknown halide is **chloride**",
    "reading_order" : 66,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000067",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "If the precipitate does not dissolve in dilute but in **concentrated **ammonia the unknown halide is **bromide**",
    "reading_order" : 67,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000068",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "If the precipitate does not dissolve in **dilute **nor **concentrated **ammonia the unknown halide is iodide",
    "reading_order" : 68,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
